{
  "utterance": "Nico, your zero case is the best test so far, because it makes the learner find which step breaks. That looks like giving a reason, even if the speech is halting. So I now think explanation isn't what I need; I need evidence that the learner can defend the step against a challenge. A cook who pulls back the stock and points at the wet rice may do that without a lecture. Then my worry is this: if a demonstration counts as a reason, what stops any successful adjustment from counting? What would separate defending from lucky repair?",
  "cites": ["IR1"],
  "memory": "Revised: verbal explanation is not necessary; evidence of reasons is, such as locating the breaking step or showing the cause. Worry: successful adjustment alone might be luck. Next: what distinguishes a demonstrated reason from a repeated lucky repair?"
}
